// Title: Arbitrary Shell Command Execution Via Settingcontent-Ms
// Sigma ID: 24de4f3b-804c-4165-b442-5a06a2302c7e
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Sreeman
// Date: 2020-03-13 | Modified: 2022-04-14
// ATT&CK: T1204, T1566.001, execution, initial-access
// Description: The .SettingContent-ms file type was introduced in Windows 10 and allows a user to create
//   "shortcuts" to various Windows 10 setting pages. These files are simply XML and contain paths to
//   various Windows 10 settings binaries.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://posts.specterops.io/the-tale-of-settingcontent-ms-files-f1ea253e4d39
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_susp_arbitrary_shell_execution_via_settingcontent.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ProcessCommandLine contains ".SettingContent-ms" and (not(ProcessCommandLine contains "immersivecontrolpanel"))
